WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.189
Hi, welcome back to our at-home lam rim (LAM RIM) retreat.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.040
Thanks again to Stanley for coming back--you didn't run away!

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.100
And we're on session 20, day number 20.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.560
And the title of this session is called 
"A Self-Perpetuating Wheel of Pain."

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:51.020
And the image we're using 
is a downward cycle staircase.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.220
The word "self-perpetuating" is very important.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:10.640
I had a watch made recently by a Swiss friend

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:24.820
for Veronica. 
It took him a year to make it, so it's a very nice watch.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.740
I was trying to figure out this winding thing.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:42.120
It took a year, it cost a lot of money,
and I called him I said, "it's not working."

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:53.100
He said, "What's wrong with it?"  
I said, "I'm trying to wind it and it's not working."

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.420
He said, "Geshe-la, it's a self-winding watch."

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:11.340
"What's a self-winding watch?" He said, "Every time you move, it winds it again. It winds itself."

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.900
And sansara (Saṃsāra), let's talk about this word "sansara."

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:30.400
First of all, the correct pronunciation is "sansara," 
like "S-A-N" not "S-A-M".

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:45.960
You hear a lot of people say "samsara." 
And that's a mistake based on the English letters 
used to show Sanskrit.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.640
But according to the rules of Sanskrit

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.660
before an "S" an "M" changes to an "N".

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.400
So we should say "sansara."

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:14.760
And you know you tell people, 
"How are you doing today Stanley?"

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:27.500
"Samsara is being bad to me. This samsara is really bad. I'm thinking to move to Hawaii."

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:35.220
That's not sansara, okay, and this world is not sansara. Get used to it.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.700
This world is not sansara.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.980
Sansara is a kind of self-winding mechanism.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:56.420
You get trouble,
which comes from doing something bad.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:12.440
To get out of the trouble you do something bad, then you get more trouble, and that becomes a cycle.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:20.580
To talk about it in real terms, 
in the two husbands example,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.460
the wife--okay, it could be the husband, 
but we just use the wife--

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.880
the wife yells at the kids on Friday.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.160
She hears her words, they plant a seed in her mind.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:48.540
Next Wednesday that seed opens
 when she walks in the kitchen

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.840
and her husband says, "You're stupid."

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.000
Then automatically she says, 
"I didn't say anything and he called me stupid!"

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:07.440
"I didn't do anything!" How many thousand times have you said these words?

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.240
"I didn't do anything, and my wife yelled at me."

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.160
I do something, she doesn't yell at me.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.940
Just kidding.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.040
Then she yells back.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:29.620
"I just got home! I didn't say anything!
You attacked me, called me stupid."

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:36.060
Then because she doesn't understand, 
she gives an answer, "I'm not stupid, you're stupid."

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:46.340
She hears her own words, they plant a seed in her mind.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:52.020
The next week that seed opens in the kids' bedroom,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.040
the kids are bad.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.100
She says, "I didn't do anything. The kids are bad."

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.420
And then that is a sansara.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:09.220
It's one self-winding watch,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.560
and you have thousands going on at the same time.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:24.080
You can even yell at somebody in your office at work

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:31.900
and that seed will open at home, 20 miles away,

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.920
and your husband will yell at you.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.840
"Oh, Geshe-la, I didn't know I can make a bad seed at work.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.020
It will open in my kitchen at home?"

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.540
Yes.
"Why?"

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:57.420
You brought the bad seed 
in the suitcase [in your mind] from home. 
You brought it home and it opened there.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.680
So in this session, number 20,

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:12.480
we're going talk about the idea that
 sansara tends to create more sansara.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.520
Let's go to the text.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:25.420
tendrel di dak nga-ma nga-ma le, chi-ma chi-ma kye ne
(RTEN 'BREL 'DI DAG SNGA MA SNGA MA LAS, 
PHYI MA PHYI MA BSKYED NAS)

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.200
So, sets of cause and effect--

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:37.220
earlier sets of cause and effect--
can also trigger later sets of cause and effect.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.720
kyewa ga shi sok dukngel tamche kormor nyongwa yin
(SKYE BA RGA SHI SOGS SDUG BSNGAL 
THAMS CAD 'KHOR MOR MYONG BA YIN)

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.360
Therefore, it tends to repeat itself.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.960
Reborn.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:54.540
Get old.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.040
Die.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.540
During the reborn you did more seeds,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:06.220
kormor ('KHOR MOR) means it "happens in cycles"--

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:11.640
big sansaras that take three lifetimes,

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:19.520
and little sansaras with your
kids and your husband that take three days.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.840
le chik gi drebuy tendrel gyi yun la
(LAS GCIG GI 'BRAS BU'I RTEN 'BREL GYI YUN LA)

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.940
Now, she yells at her kids on Friday.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:44.420
And then on Wednesday when she comes home,
that seed opens and her husband yells at her.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.620
During the yelling by the husband, 
which is a result of a seed opening,

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:11.120
she's getting upset and new seeds are being planted at the same five minutes that the old seeds are opening.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.880
At the end of one mini sansara,

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.680
three, four, five more sansaras are being triggered.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.840
So it's very important to think about that.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:58.520
Listen!  
Buddhism says every cause is also a result. 
And every result is also a cause for something else.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:08.300
And in the Abhidharma, they struggled to find some cause which is not a result or some result which is not a cause.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:19.660
They like to say the last moment 
before you be reached Nirvana.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:33.380
That's a result, but it's not a cause because they believe you disappear when you reach nirvana.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:43.440
But that's a famous example 
of a result which is not a cause.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.040
But it's a wrong example.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.980
So while she's getting yelled at by the husband,

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.320
at the same moment she's making new causes.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:04.460
kye shing, ga shi kyewa le jungwa ne
SKYE ZHING, RGA SHI SKYE BA LAS 'BYUNG BA NAS)

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:09.440
Aging and death continue to happen from rebirth,

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:20.700
and then misunderstanding your life 
is continuing to make seeds.

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:29.760
Therefore, 
chik gi chik drang ne korway korlo korway tsawa ni marikpa nyi yin
(GCIG GIS GCIG DRANGS NAS 'KHOR BA'I 'KHOR LO 'KHOR BA'I RTZA BA NI MA RIG PA NYID YIN)

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:37.340
So if we dig, dig, dig, 
and find the single cause of all of them,

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:51.800
marikpa nyi (MA RIG PA NYID). marikpa (MA RIG PA) means "misunderstanding,"
and nyi (NYID) means "just that one thing."

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:58.900
Now, you know, I've been studying Buddhism 
for fifty years or something.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.000
Hard. I mean, many hours a day.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:15.220
These ideas, just that example in the kitchen,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.640
that's all you need.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:27.440
Let's say there's a geshe in the monastery,

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.960
studied thirty, forty, fifty years.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.580
They studied all the "jing," all the sutras,

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:42.480
and they know all the philosophy, 
they know all the schools.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:56.040
Or there's one woman working 
for some company, and she comes home

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:03.780
and her husband says," you're stupid," 
and immediately she thinks

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:13.580
"Oh, that's happening because I planted a seed 
when I yelled at my kids last week."

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:24.040
That one idea, in one woman's mind in one kitchen--

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:40.300
she just understood all the sutras about dependent origination, and there's nothing more than that.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:51.280
And if you ask her, "that husband yelling at you,

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:01.020
did he decide to yell at you? 
Is that why you're getting yelled at?"

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.340
If that woman says,

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:10.660
"No, no, no, no. No, not like that. He's coming from me."

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:17.720
"No, but I see some husband yelling at you, 
who doesn't come from you."

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:25.540
Then if she says "No, no, that one doesn't exist. 
It was always a wrong idea.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:36.020
I thought he decided to yell at me, 
but now I understand he is just coming from me.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:49.420
I used to think there was a husband in the kitchen who, just before I got home, decided to yell at me.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:58.840
But then I understood dependent origination

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:06.500
and I understood he comes from me 
yelling at my kids last week.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.140
And there's only that one husband in my kitchen.

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.720
Only one, who comes from me"

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:24.920
What percent of their husband comes from you?

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:34.280
100.000 percent of that husband comes from me.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:42.840
Therefore, there's no other husband in the kitchen

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:50.680
who decided to yell at me just before I got home.

00:15:53.360 --> 00:16:04.580
That woman who says that husband 
is 100.000 percent coming from me,

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:19.260
she understands dependent origination, and when she goes like this... and there's no other husband in the kitchen,

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:23.240
she just saw emptiness.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:30.920
And she's a master of dependent origination, 
and she is a master of emptiness.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:47.540
Better than a geshe in the monastery who spent forty years studying dependent origination sutras and emptiness sutras who doesn't understand the husband.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:05.660
So, I think it's important to learn 
all of the backup material,

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.600
but that doesn't make you a master.

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:16.900
Master of emptiness. Master of dependent origination.

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:25.660
Any normal person who understands why their wife 
or their husband is yelling at them,

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:34.680
and who understands there's no other husband or wife,

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:47.740
they are a master of dependent origination. 
They are a master of emptiness, and they have mastered the teaching of the Buddha, all of it.

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:56.040
Let's get back to the book.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:02.880
We saw many, many wheels affecting... 
like inside a watch.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:10.060
And we have that image, 
I'm going to show it to you again.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:15.800
These different cogs, interacting gears.

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:24.640
I have a crush. I have a crush on Tsongkapa, 
I have a crush on my lama.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:30.600
Nowadays, I especially have a crush on Arya Nagarjuna.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:41.180
And here's my favorite picture of Nagarjuna.

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:49.000
I really like it. I don't know, he just looks really cool.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:57.620
We are translating some of Nagarjuna's works 
in our Mixed Nuts translation group.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:16.360
The director of the Mixed Nuts translator group, 
Nick Lashaw, he's translating one of Nagarjuna's most difficult works,

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.220
called the "Sixty Verses."

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:34.300
And it's basically a long essay, 
in poetry, about emptiness.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:43.080
So again, you're very welcome to go look at these books.

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:52.520
What's it called? Asian... 
Diamondcutterclassics.com.
.org? .com?

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:55.880
.com

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:06.020
You can go read his commentary/translation for free.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:16.580
And at the YouTube Tibetan Language channel,

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:24.600
there's hundreds of classes about Nagarjuna.

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.940
So you should go see them.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:33.900
Here, our author Pabongka Rinpoche

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:43.980
quotes Arya Nagarjuna, and as usual it's poetry.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:50.863
sumpo dak le nyi jung te,
nyi le dun jung dun le kyang,
sum jung sipay korlo de,
nyi ni yang dang yang du kor

00:20:50.863 --> 00:20:56.800
(,GSUM PO DAG LAS GNYIS 'BYUNG STE,
,GNYIS LAS BDUN 'BYUNG BDUN LAS KYANG;
;GSUM 'BYUNG SRID PA'I 'KHOR LO DE,
,NYID NI YANG DANG YANG DU 'KHOR,)

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:16.100
"Two come from the three. Seven come from the two. And from the seven, again three."

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:27.860
It's a wheel of pain, and nothing more, 
and it circles, and it circles, and it circles.

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:32.260
Okay, now he has a commentary.

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:45.740
nyon mong sum (NYON MONGS GSUM) means 
"three of the links are negative emotions."

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:57.520
These are one, eight, and nine. 
Number one is misunderstanding your husband.

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:06.480
Number eight, the simple way you can say it is:
"Are you sorry you yelled at your husband?"  
And you say, "No."

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:16.180
And number nine is like: 
"Does the husband come from you or from him?"

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:24.440
"Oh, I thought about it and I think he comes from him."

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:38.460
So those are three negative emotions. They produce two. What's "two"? "Two" means two kinds of karma.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.820
Link number two and link number ten.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:51.120
Link number two is fresh karma, like the watermelon seed you just put under the ground.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:03.500
And link number ten is the watermelon seed that got the water and got the fertilizer and it's nice and fat and it's going to open soon.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:16.000
We've been talking about 
how to group the twelve into groups.

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:31.260
Now we have Nagarjuna's grouping.
And by the way, Nagarjuna wrote an incredible tiny book, one or two pages, about the twelve links.

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:50.420
It's called "Pratitya Samutpada Hirdaya Karika (Pratītya samutpāda hṝdaya kārika), which means "Verses about the Very Heart 
of Dependent Origination."

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.980
He said let's group it this way.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:04.980
In the twelve links, three of them are negative emotions.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:08.720
Number one, misunderstanding.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:14.960
Number eight--like light and... how do you say it?

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:20.320
We can say "being happy about your bad seeds."

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:29.020
And then number nine, totally not understanding those bad seeds and giving them more fertilizer.

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:37.140
So in Nagarjuna's way of grouping the twelve,

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:41.840
three of them are negative emotions.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:49.160
What's your second group Nagarjuna? "Oh, that's the two of them belong together because they're karma."

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:56.960
There's the skinny seed 
that you first put under the ground--

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:01.640
"I'm not stupid, you're stupid"--

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:12.200
and then there's the big fat seed 
that's ready to produce some bad result for you. 
For example, next week your kids will not behave.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:21.920
Then, one of the things I love about Nagarjuna the most,

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:31.700
he understood what Miles Davis said. Miles Davis is the greatest jazz player of all time, maybe.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:48.360
And his most famous quotation about jazz: 
"Less is more."

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:52.460
So it's not how many notes you can play,

00:25:53.720 --> 00:26:00.300
it's about what notes do you leave out, 
and the other notes become more powerful.

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:10.080
And Nagarjuna is unbelievable 
at giving less which becomes more.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:22.760
"Hey Nagarjuna, we had all these groups, twelve, 
you only have two so far."

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:34.880
Three of the twelve links are negative emotions. 
Two of the twelve links are karma.

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:43.740
"What about the other seven?
"Huh? Other seven? I got one word: pain."

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:50.240
So, he's like this... three are negative emotions,

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:53.460
two are karma,

00:26:54.019 --> 00:26:59.529
seven are-- he says a bad word--seven are just pain.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.740
Let's review what those seven are.

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:09.040
Link number three--your mind going into your mom.

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:15.700
Link number four--your body and mind inside your mom.

00:27:16.580 --> 00:27:22.440
Link number five--your sense organs growing.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:29.000
Link number six--they make contact 
with the outside world, and the inside world.

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:37.960
Link number eleven--getting born in this kind of place.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:45.940
Link number twelve--getting old and dying in this way.

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:51.580
So then I kind of see it as a conversation.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.780
I think Nagarjuna is in a Starbucks,

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:03.460
he's just drinking coffee and talking with his friend,

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:09.440
and his friends is like, "Yeah, so you covered all twelve."

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:19.800
Three negative emotions, two karma, 
and seven complete poop in your life.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:28.960
And his friend is like, "Yeah Nagarjuna--[sips coffee]--you got anything else to say?"

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:32.860
"Yeah, I'll tell you something really bad.

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:40.480
The seven poop links cause the three 
negative emotion links, again!"

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:52.960
So now Nagarjuna took us to a new place, and it's so short you almost miss how significant it is.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:06.240
Yeah, three negative feelings, 
they make you make karma.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:14.720
Then if you make karma, you get this 
body and mind, and suffer, suffer, suffer.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:27.180
Oh, and suffer, suffer, suffer--  
they make another set of three bad thoughts.

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:32.460
And those make another two karmas,

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:40.100
and those make another seven, 
and they make another three,
and they make another two,
and they make another seven....

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:49.640
jungwa sok ngar shin korwe sipay korlo gyun mi chepar kor
('BYUNG BA SOGS SNGAR BZHIN 'KHOR BAS SRID PA'I 'KHOR LO RGYUN MI 'CHAD PAR 'KHOR)--

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:55.040
therefore, it is a self perpetuating wheel.

00:29:56.220 --> 00:30:01.740
And it has been turning for time with no beginning without a single break.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:12.620
And as long as those twelve links circle,
we will have pain.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:22.100
Okay, that's the very cheerful session number 20.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:31.400
Thanks again to Stanley for translating 
and we'll see you tomorrow at session 21,

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:38.360
which is called "Seeing that the Jail is a Jail.

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:43.800
See you tomorrow.

